 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "NORTH YORK MOORS RAILWAY. Opened in 1836, shut in 1965 and brought back by volunteers who clearly enjoy being sooty. A film about a school for wizards used one of its stations, painted for the camera."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "LEVEL CROSSING. Look both ways. Then look both ways again. Then, if you hear a whistle, look at the sky, because the steam train has a plume that you will see first."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 54,
   "text": "THE STONES. A ring of weathered stones on the moor top, put there by people who never wrote down why. The moors are full of burial mounds and cairns. Sit, and listen to the wind do its stone-flavoured whistling."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE HOLE OF HORCUM. A bowl in the moor 400 feet deep. Legend says a giant called Wade scooped up a handful of earth to throw at his wife, and it became Blakey Topping. Mrs Wade, by every account, had a good arm."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 4,
   "plaque": "north_york_moors_lilla",
   "title": "Lilla Cross",
   "text": "Lilla Cross, high on Fylingdales Moor, is thought to be among the oldest Christian monuments in the north. Tradition links it to Lilla, a thegn who, Bede tells us, threw himself between King Edwin and an assassin's blade in 626 and died saving him."
  },
  {
   "x": 68,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "north_york_moors_rosedale",
   "title": "Rosedale Ironstone",
   "text": "In the 19th century Rosedale was busy with miners digging ironstone, and a railway ran right round the high moor to carry it off to the ironworks of the North East. The great stone kilns where the ore was roasted still stand on the hillside, now kept by sheep."
  }
 ],
